{"name":"Suit Blue","hex":"#2b3036","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"suit","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A set of clothes to be worn together, now especially a man's matching jacket and trousers (also business suit or lounge suit), or a similar outfit for a woman.","A garment or set of garments suitable and/or required for a given task or activity: space suit, boiler suit, protective suit, swimsuit."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make proper or suitable; to adapt or fit.","To be suitable or apt for one's image."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/suit"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Suit","blocks":[{"text":"A suit, also called a lounge suit, business suit, dress suit, or formal suit, is a set of clothes comprising a suit jacket and trousers of identical textiles generally worn with a collared dress shirt, necktie, and dress shoes. A skirt suit is similar, but with a matching pencil skirt instead of trousers. It is currently considered semi-formal wear or business wear in contemporary Western dress codes; however, when the suit was originally developed it was considered an informal or more casual option compared to the prevailing clothing standards of aristocrats and businessmen. The lounge suit originated in 19th-century Britain as sportswear and British country clothing, which is why it was seen as more casual than citywear at that time, with the roots of the suit coming from early modern Western Europe formal court or military clothes. After replacing the black frock coat in the early 20th century as regular daywear, a sober one-coloured suit became known as a lounge suit."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Matt_Smith_on_%22Christopher_And_His_Kind%22_set.jpg/330px-Matt_Smith_on_%22Christopher_And_His_Kind%22_set.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suit","wikidata":"Q614806"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Gentleman_in_bowler.JPG/330px-Gentleman_in_bowler.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Gentleman_in_bowler.JPG/330px-Gentleman_in_bowler.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A man dressed in a three-piece suit and bowler hat","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGentleman_in_bowler.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Three_Piece_%28SBW%29_Tweed_%E2%80%93_Yellow-Olive-Taupe_Windowpa